TK=[
'**Mechanical coupling:** Tympanic displacement drives receptor excitation; in the measured noctuid preparation, A1 and A2 differ substantially in displacement threshold.',
'**Range and timing:** A1 is more sensitive than A2, but short calls can saturate A1 before A2 recruitment. Pulse duration and repetition influence the information reaching central neurons.',
'**Central transformation:** Repeaters preserve much of an afferent burst, whereas pulse-marker units select event timing. Their transmitters and complete causal escape circuit were not established by the extracellular recordings.',
'**Escape geometry:** Moths can survive faster bats through sharply timed turns and trajectories toward the predator’s flanks. Radial acceleration and escape angle predict outcomes better than speed alone.',
'**Acoustic defense:** Tiger-moth clicks can jam bat sonar. Sound-organ silencing and persistent protection across repeated attacks provide causal and comparative evidence for this defense.',
'**Evidence limits:** Hearing a sound, encoding its intensity and executing escape are different outcomes. A neural threshold or a model prediction alone does not prove the corresponding defensive behavior.'
]
